- op: discuss  # f4b3
  at: '2026-08-28T09:54:25Z'  # f4b3
  by: agent  # f4b3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f4b3
  clock: 5  # f4b3
  nonce: f4b3  # f4b3
  message: "DONE. PR #603, dev e40b7df4ac. Took the \"ranking is cheapest\" direction this\nitem recommended.\n\nFIX. _rank_modules_for_disclosure sorts third-party first, alphabetical within\neach group, applied before truncation at both module-list sites (uncatalogued\nmodules and method-starved modules). On the item's own fixture -- ten stdlib\nmodules plus requests -- `requests` now leads the list instead of sitting in\nthe \"+7 more\".\n\nNOT applied to opaque launch SITES: those are commands and paths, not modules,\nso is_stdlib_module has nothing to say about them and asking would invent an\norder rather than reveal one.\n\nON USING is_stdlib_module AT ALL, since LIVE.md flags it as RECOGNITION not\nprovenance and it cost a P0 (INV-buzab). That failure was using it to decide\nwhether a module had been EXAMINED. This is a DISPLAY ORDER: no verdict\nchanges, the count is unchanged, the full list stays in the caveat's machine\nsurface. Its failure mode also points the right way -- a stdlib module the\nrecogniser has not enumerated sorts as third-party, and the unfamiliar name is\nexactly the one a reader most needs to see. Pinned as a test.\n\nREFACTOR: the \"a, b, c (+N more)\" rendering was written out three times, each\nrepeating the cap arithmetic; now one helper, so the three disclosures cannot\ndrift in how they say it."  # f4b3
